 | Voice | The owner's FIRST Play (`GeneratePill` in `ExplanationAudio.jsx` calls the host's `narrate`) | `POST /api/learn/explanation-audio/voice` `{ app, id, script, confirmed: true }` -> `{ narration }` | ElevenLabs `eleven_flash_v2_5`, voice Jessica (`cgSgspJ2msm6clMCkdW9`), `stability 0.4, similarity_boost 0.75, speed 1.1`, `mp3_44100_64` | `block.narration = { status: 'ready', fileRef, seconds, script, transcript, provider: 'elevenlabs', model: 'eleven_flash_v2_5', voiceId }`, or `{ status: 'error', failed: 'audio', code, reason }` with the script kept |
 
 - **Fields.** `script` is the only text the voice gets; `transcript` is the same sentences with every equation and number as LaTeX between dollars (what the transcript window shows). `seconds` is a words / 3 estimate until the voice exists, then the file's own length (bytes at 64 kbps). `scriptBy` is the script model; `provider`, `model` and `voiceId` are the voice. The server writes no board: the client lands each answer on the card (`explanation-audio.js` `landNarration`, a quiet update), and the board's normal save registers `narration.fileRef` (`file-refs.js`), so a share or publication link plays it.
-- **Length by Depth.** Spoken target: Brief about 60 to 80 s (180 to 240 words), Balanced about 90 s (240 to 300), Detailed up to about 2 minutes (300 to 360), stated plainly in the prompt (no "soft", no "never pad", no "stop when the idea is complete"). The script model is told the card's own Depth (`block.depth`). Gemini writes about 0.6 / 0.7 / 0.9 of the words it is asked for (length measurement of 2026-11-17: 144 real calls, raw medians 129 / 205 / 318 words), so its asks are higher (Brief 300 to 360, Balanced 360 to 420, Detailed 400 to 460 words, with a content checklist and "do not stop before N words" for all three); Fireworks gets the plain targets.
+- **Length by Depth.** Spoken target: Brief about 60 to 80 s (180 to 240 words), Balanced about 90 s (240 to 300), Detailed up to about 2 minutes (300 to 360), stated plainly in the prompt (no "soft", no "never pad", no "stop when the idea is complete"). The script model is told the card's own Depth (`block.depth`). Gemini writes about 0.6 / 0.7 / 0.9 of the words it is asked for (length measurement of 2026-11-17: 144 real calls, raw medians 129 / 205 / 318 words), so its asks are higher (Brief 300 to 360, Balanced 360 to 420, Detailed 360 to 420 words and "never above 420 words" (a 400 to 460 ask overshot: median 341, max 507 words, about 170 s), with a content checklist and "do not stop before N words" for all three); Fireworks gets the plain targets.
 - **Hard floors and the one re-ask (owner 2026-11-17, superseding "no model retry in v1").** After the number check, the kept script must have at least 110 (Brief), 200 (Balanced) or 300 (Detailed) words AND speak at least one of the card's own data numbers, meaning a number the card's PROSE or the learner's question states (a card whose prose has none, only counting words, or only numbers inside a diagram, asks for no example; measured: six of the 12 bench cards had a JSON `version: 1` as their only number). A script that fails either gets exactly ONE re-ask: a single extra call to the same model with the same prompt, its first answer, and one user turn that names the failed rule(s) (`too short: N words after removing sentences with outside numbers; target L to H words; use the card's own numbers.` and/or `none of the card's own numbers (...) is spoken; say at least one of them aloud in the explanation`). Still under the floor after it, the script step is an error (`too_short`, the retry pill), never short audio. The card-number rule only triggers the re-ask: still missing after it, the script ships (outcome ok) with `check.exampleMissing: true`. The answer and the log carry `check` = `{ reasked, firstRawWords, firstStrippedWords, firstFailed?, rawWords, strippedWords, failed }` (the client keeps it as `narration.scriptCheck`); a provider failing on the re-ask hands the card to the next provider, which has no re-ask left.
 - **Style.** The script mirrors the card's own register, vocabulary and rhythm (the prompt's STYLE section); no added enthusiasm.
 - **Prompt.** `explanation-audio-script.js` `scriptSystem` is AUD-2's v1.1 (`docs/features/explain/checks/aud-2/prompt-v1.1.txt`, root causes in `why.md`) with these changes: the numbers rule is the owner's (only numbers that appear in the card or its visuals, never compute; this ends v1.1's tension with its reuse rule); the answer is `{script, transcript}`; Detailed stops at about 2 minutes; the LaTeX delimiter is written by code so the source carries no money-looking text.
